t) to \"Clinician Time Saved.\" This directly maps to the financial pain points hospitals currently face due to physician burnout and administrative overhead.\n\n**Data Quality Note:** The path to feasibility requires moving beyond the \"prototype\" mindset. By institutionalizing liability management, supporting legacy data ingestion, and utilizing a model-agnostic architecture, NEXUS-Health can mitigate the primary risks that cause startups in this space to stall during security audits."}, {"role": "user", "name": "user", "content": "Refine the plan based on the critique to strengthen the value proposition and strategic approach.\n\n--- Outputs from all previous steps ---\n[Step 1 \u2014 PLANNER]\nAs the Planner of NEXUS AI, I have architected a structured business plan for an AI-powered healthcare startup, leveraging the principles of modular agent orchestration and scalable backend architecture established in our previous projects.\n\n# Business Plan: \"NEXUS-Health\" \u2014 AI-Driven Clinical Support Platform\n\n## Phase 1: Research & Problem Definition\n1.  **Define the Core Problem:** Identify the \"Physician Burnout & Diagnostic Latency\" gap. Focus on the time clinicians spend on administrative documentation and preliminary symptom analysis.\n2.  **Market Validation:** Conduct a competitive analysis of current EHR (Electronic Health Record) integration tools.\n3.  **Regulatory Scoping:** Research compliance requirements (HIPAA, GDPR, HITECH) to ensure data sovereignty and patient privacy from day one.\n\n## Phase 2: Technical Architecture (NEXUS Modular Approach)\n4.  **Agent Orchestrator:** Design a centralized Orchestrator agent to manage traffic between the Frontend, Medical Database, and Analysis Agents.\n5.  **Specialized Agent Roles:**\n    *   *Triage Agent:* Processes initial patient input.\n    *   *Data Synthesis Agent:* Summarizes medical history from existing EHRs.\n    *   *Compliance Agent:* Monitors outputs for HIPAA-compliant logging and PII 