lucinations\" in Diagnosis.**\n    *   *Mitigation:* Implement a \"Human-in-the-loop\" (HITL) mandatory check for all diagnostic summaries; the AI only suggests, it does not confirm.\n*   **Failure Point 3: Integration Friction with Legacy EHRs.**\n    *   *Mitigation:* Develop standardized FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources) API connectors to bridge the gap with existing software systems.\n\n---\n\n**Next Steps:** Would you like me to generate the specific technical stack architecture for the `ai-inference-service`?\n\n[Step 2 \u2014 RESEARCHER]\n[Web Search Results for: 'current trends in AI healthcare 2024 2025']\n\n1. Generative AI in healthcare: Current trends and future outlook\n   The latest survey, conducted in the fourth quarter of2024, found that 85 percent of respondents\u2014healthcare leaders from payers, health systems, andhealthcareservices and technology (HST) groups\u2014were exploring or had already adopted genAIcapabilities.\n   Source: https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/healthcare/our-insights/generative-ai-in-healthcare-current-trends-and-future-outlook\n\n2. 2025: The State of AI in Healthcare | Menlo Ventures\n   HealthcareAIspending hit $1.4 billion this year, 7 nearly tripling2024'sinvestment. To put that in perspective, our2024EnterpriseAIreport 8 estimated the entire verticalAImarket, including sectors from law to design and breakout companies like Harvey, Eve *, Midjourney, and Higgsfield *, at just $1.2 billion last year.\n   Source: https://menlovc.com/perspective/2025-the-state-of-ai-in-healthcare/\n\n3. Adoption of artificial intelligence in healthcare: survey of health ...\n   Abstract Importance The UShealthcaresystem faces significant challenges, including clinician burnout, operational inefficiencies, and concerns about patient safety. Artificial intelligence (AI), particularly generativeAI, has the potential to address these challenges, but its adoption, effectiveness, and barriers to implementation are not well un